"What is a covariant return type?","A method in a subclass can return a subtype of the return type declared in the superclass method.","A method that can return multiple different types.","A method whose return type is determined at runtime.","A method that must return the exact same type as the overridden superclass method.","A"
"Which of these is the primary reason to override both `equals()` and `hashCode()`?","To ensure that objects that are equal have the same hash code, as required by hash-based collections.","To improve the performance of object comparison.","To allow objects to be used in `if` statements.","To provide a unique identifier for every object.","A"
"What is type erasure in Java generics?","The process of enforcing type constraints at runtime.","The process by which the compiler removes generic type information after compilation.","The automatic casting of generic types.","A runtime error caused by incorrect generic types.","B"
"Which of the following correctly uses a bounded wildcard to accept a List of any subclass of Number?","`List<? extends Number> list`","`List<? super Number> list`","`List<T extends Number> list`","`List<?> list`","A"
"What is the main difference between a `Callable` and a `Runnable`?","`Callable` can return a value and throw a checked exception, while `Runnable` cannot.","`Runnable` can return a value, while `Callable` cannot.","`Callable` is used for single-threaded applications, `Runnable` is for multi-threaded.","`Callable` has a `start()` method, `Runnable` has a `run()` method.","A"
"What is the purpose of the `try-with-resources` statement?","To automatically close resources that implement the `AutoCloseable` interface.","To allocate more resources to a `try` block.","A more efficient way to catch exceptions.","To try acquiring a resource multiple times before failing.","A"
"Which memory area in the JVM stores objects and instance variables?","Stack","Heap","Method Area","PC Register","B"
"Which of these is a terminal operation in the Java Stream API?","`map()`","`filter()`","`sorted()`","`forEach()`","D"
"What is a static nested class?","A class defined inside another class that can access instance members of the outer class.","A class defined inside a method.","A class that is automatically imported.","A class defined inside another class that cannot access instance members of theouter class without an explicit reference.","D"
"What does the `Integer.valueOf(127) == Integer.valueOf(127)` expression evaluate to?","true","false","It depends on the JVM.","Compilation Error","A"
"Which design pattern ensures a class has only one instance and provides a global point of access to it?","Factory","Abstract Factory","Singleton","Builder","C"
"What is reflection in Java?","The process of mirroring an object's state for backup.","The ability of a program to inspect and modify its own structure and behavior at runtime.","A technique for creating deep copies of objects.","A way to handle GUI events.","B"
"Which statement is true about `String` objects in the Java string pool?","All `String` objects are stored in the string pool.","Only string literals are automatically placed in the string pool.","`new String(""text"")` will always use the string pool.","The string pool is part of the stack memory.","B"
"What is the `ExecutorService` framework used for?","Managing a pool of threads to execute tasks concurrently.","Executing system-level commands.","Managing database connections.","Providing network services.","A"
"Which of the following is NOT a valid state for a thread?","NEW","RUNNABLE","WAITING","FINISHED","D"
"Which of these is a key benefit of using an `enum` over static final constants?","Enums are more memory efficient.","Enums provide type safety.","Enums can be subclassed.","Enums can be modified at runtime.","B"
"What is the function of a `java.util.Comparator`?","To define the natural ordering of a class.","To define a custom, external ordering for objects of a class.","To compare two primitive data types.","To ensure objects are equal.","B"
"In the context of generics, what does `List<Object>` mean?","A list that can hold any type of object.","A list that can hold objects of an unknown type.","A supertype of all other `List` types (e.g., `List<String>`).","A list that can only hold instances of the `Object` class itself.","D"
"What is a deadlock in multithreading?","A situation where a thread is waiting indefinitely for a resource.","A situation where two or more threads are blocked forever, waiting for each other.","An error that occurs when a thread tries to access a null object.","A race condition where data is corrupted.","B"
"Which of the following provides thread-safe access to a `HashMap`?","`TreeMap`","`LinkedHashMap`","`ConcurrentHashMap`","`HashSet`","C"
"What does the `@Override` annotation do?","It indicates that the annotated method is an override of a superclass method, causing a compile error if it is not.","It prevents a method from being overridden.","It specifies that a method is deprecated.","It automatically provides the method implementation from the superclass.","A"
"What is the difference between `Files.readAllBytes()` and `Files.lines()`?","`readAllBytes()` reads the entire file into memory as a byte array, while `lines()` reads it lazily as a `Stream<String>`.","`readAllBytes()` is for text files, `lines()` is for binary files.","`readAllBytes()` is an older, deprecated method.","There is no significant difference; they are aliases.","A"
"Which of these is an intermediate operation in the Java Stream API?","`collect()`","`reduce()`","`count()`","`filter()`","D"
"What is a method reference in Java 8?","A reference to an object's memory location.","A pointer to a method in C++.","A shorthand syntax for a lambda expression that executes a single method.","A way to document a method.","C"
"Which collection is most efficient for implementing a LIFO (Last-In, First-Out) stack?","`ArrayList`","`PriorityQueue`","`ArrayDeque`","`LinkedList`","C"
"What is the purpose of the `volatile` keyword?","It ensures that changes to a variable are always visible to other threads.","It makes a variable constant.","It prevents a variable from being garbage collected.","It marks a variable for serialization.","A"
"Which of the following is an example of a 'phantom reference'?","A reference that is cleared as soon as the object is no longer strongly reachable.","A reference used for scheduling post-mortem cleanup actions, enqueued after an object is finalized.","A reference that does not prevent an object from being garbage collected, but allows for some action while it is still in memory.","A strong reference to an object.","B"
"What is the `java.nio` package primarily used for?","Networking operations.","Non-blocking I/O operations.","Numeric operations.","New object instantiation.","B"
"What is the output of this stream operation? `Stream.of(1, 2, 3).reduce(10, (a, b) -> a + b)`","6","16","15","Compilation Error","B"
"What is the 'fail-fast' behavior of an iterator in the Java Collections Framework?","It throws a `ConcurrentModificationException` if the underlying collection is modified during iteration by a means other than the iterator's own methods.","It fails quickly if the collection is empty.","It pre-validates all elements before starting iteration.","It ensures the iteration will always complete successfully.","A"
"What is the difference between `Statement` and `PreparedStatement` in JDBC?","`PreparedStatement` is pre-compiled and can prevent SQL injection attacks.","`Statement` is newer and more efficient.","`PreparedStatement` can only be used for `SELECT` queries.","There is no difference in functionality, only in name.","A"
"Which is the correct way to obtain a `Class` object for the `String` class?","`String.class`","`""hello"".getClass()`","`Class.forName(""java.lang.String"")`","All of the above","D"
"What is the principle of 'composition over inheritance'?","Favoring creating complex objects by including instances of other classes, rather than inheriting their functionality.","Favoring inheriting from multiple classes to create a new one.","A design principle that states inheritance is always better.","A rule that classes should always be composed of primitive types.","A"
"Which of the following is true about a `finally` block?","It is executed only when an exception is thrown.","It will not execute if `System.exit()` is called in the `try` or `catch` block.","It can be used without a `catch` block.","It must be placed after all `catch` blocks.","B"
"What is the role of a `ClassLoader` in the JVM?","To compile `.java` files into `.class` files.","To load `.class` files into memory, verify them, and prepare them for execution.","To manage the garbage collection process.","To interpret Java bytecode into native machine code.","B"
"In Java, a `ReentrantLock` provides an alternative to which keyword?","`volatile`","`final`","`static`","`synchronized`","D"
"What does the `Optional.orElse()` method do?","It returns the contained value if present, otherwise it returns a provided default value.","It throws an exception if no value is present.","It executes a `Consumer` if a value is present.","It returns another `Optional` if the current one is empty.","A"
"Which of the following is a 'checked' exception?","`NullPointerException`","`IllegalArgumentException`","`ClassNotFoundException`","`ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException`","C"
"What is the output? `System.out.println('j' + 'a' + 'v' + 'a');`","java","386","A string concatenation of character codes.","Compilation Error","B"
"What is the purpose of the `default` keyword in a Java 8 interface?","To specify the default implementation for a method that implementing classes can use or override.","To mark the default method to be called when an object is created.","To declare a method with default (package-private) access.","To set a default value for an interface field.","A"
"What does the 'Builder' design pattern solve?","It separates the construction of a complex object from its representation, allowing the same construction process to create different representations.","It ensures only one instance of a class is created.","It provides an interface for creating families of related objects.","It allows an object to alter its behavior when its internal state changes.","A"
"Which data structure does a `PriorityQueue` use internally?","A balanced binary search tree","A doubly-linked list","A hash table","A binary heap","D"
"What is a 'Daemon' thread?","A thread that runs in the background and does not prevent the JVM from exiting.","A malicious thread that can harm the system.","A thread with the highest possible priority.","The main thread of an application.","A"
"What is the primary use case for the `transient` keyword?","To prevent a field from being included in the serialization of an object.","To mark a field as temporary and eligible for garbage collection.","To indicate a field that can change unexpectedly.","To ensure a field is initialized with a default value.","A"
"Which of the following is NOT a goal of garbage collection?","To automatically free memory occupied by objects that are no longer in use.","To prevent memory leaks.","To improve the performance of object allocation.","To guarantee that an object is destroyed as soon as it becomes unreachable.","D"
"What is a 'lambda expression'?","A block of code that can be passed around to be executed later; an anonymous function.","A regular expression used for string matching.","An annotation for generating code.","A way to define a constant value.","A"
"Which of the following is true about the `hashCode()` contract?","If two objects are equal according to `equals()`, they must have the same hash code.","If two objects have the same hash code, they must be equal according to `equals()`.","The hash code of an object can change during its lifetime.","`hashCode()` must return a unique value for every object.","A"
"What is the 'diamond problem' and how does Java 8 address it in interfaces?","It's an ambiguity from multiple inheritance, addressed by forcing implementing classes to provide their own implementation if a conflict arises with default methods.","It's a performance issue with deep inheritance, solved by making interfaces final.","It refers to memory leaks in generics, solved by type erasure.","It's an issue with circular dependencies, prevented by the compiler.","A"
"Which `Map` implementation maintains its keys in sorted order?","`HashMap`","`LinkedHashMap`","`ConcurrentHashMap`","`TreeMap`","D"
"What does the `Stream.flatMap()` operation do?","It transforms each element of a stream into another stream and then flattens the results into a single stream.","It filters out elements that do not match a predicate.","It finds the first element of a stream.","It performs a reduction on the elements of the stream.","A"
"In multithreading, what is a 'race condition'?","A situation where the outcome of a program depends on the unpredictable sequence or timing of threads accessing shared data.","A competition between threads to acquire a lock.","An error where a thread runs faster than another.","A condition where a thread is permanently blocked.","A"
"What is the difference between `wait()` and `sleep()`?","`wait()` is a method of the `Object` class and releases the lock, while `sleep()` is a static method of `Thread` and does not release the lock.","`sleep()` releases the lock, while `wait()` does not.","`wait()` is used for a specific duration, while `sleep()` is indefinite.","They are interchangeable methods with different names.","A"
"What is the `serialVersionUID` used for?","It's a version control identifier for a serializable class to verify sender and receiver compatibility.","It's a unique ID for each serialized object.","It's the serial number of the JVM.","It's used to specify the order of serialization.","A"
"Which of these is a functional interface?","An interface with exactly one abstract method.","An interface with only static methods.","An interface that cannot be implemented.","An interface used for functional programming languages.","A"
"What is the result of `""hello"".substring(5)`?","""","hello","An empty string.","`StringIndexOutOfBoundsException`","C"
"What is a 'local inner class'?","A class defined inside a method.","A class defined inside another class.","A class that is local to a package.","A static nested class.","A"
"Which statement is true regarding `abstract` classes and interfaces in Java 8+?","Interfaces can have static and default methods, while abstract classes can have state (instance variables).","Abstract classes can have static methods, but interfaces cannot.","Interfaces can be instantiated, but abstract classes cannot.","Abstract classes can implement multiple other classes.","A"
"Which of the following is true about `StringBuilder`?","It is immutable.","It is mutable and not thread-safe.","It is mutable and thread-safe.","It is a legacy class replaced by `String`.","B"
"What does the `Collector` in the Stream API's `collect()` method do?","It specifies how to accumulate stream elements into a mutable result container, such as a `List` or `Map`.","It gathers all exceptions thrown by the stream operations.","It collects metrics about the stream's performance.","It filters elements from the stream.","A"
"What is the 'PermGen' or 'Metaspace' area of the JVM used for?","Storing objects and instance variables.","Storing class metadata, method data, and static variables.","Storing thread execution stacks.","Storing native method code.","B"
"Which of these is a valid way to create a `Thread`?","By extending the `Thread` class.","By implementing the `Runnable` interface.","Both A and B.","By implementing the `Callable` interface.","C"
"What is the main advantage of using a `try-with-resources` statement?","It reduces the amount of code needed.","It guarantees that resources are closed, preventing resource leaks.","It improves the performance of I/O operations.","It allows for multiple resources to be opened at once.","B"
"What will `Optional.ofNullable(null).isPresent()` return?","true","false","It will throw a `NullPointerException`.","Compilation Error","B"
"What is the 'double-checked locking' pattern used for?","To reduce the overhead of acquiring a lock in a lazy-initialized singleton pattern.","To acquire two locks simultaneously to prevent deadlock.","To verify that a lock has been released.","To lock a variable of type double.","A"
"Which of these is NOT a principle of SOLID design?","Single Responsibility","Open/Closed","Liskov Substitution","Complex Implementation","D"
"What is the purpose of the `java.lang.instrument` package?","To allow agents to instrument programs running on the JVM.","To provide tools for musical instrument synthesis.","To define the structure of core language classes.","To manage system hardware instruments.","A"
"Which of these is the correct method signature for `equals()` in the `Object` class?","`public boolean equals(Object obj)`","`public boolean equals(String s)`","`public int equals(Object obj)`","`public void equals(Object obj)`","A"
"What is a 'bridge method' in Java?","A synthetic method created by the compiler to handle type erasure in generics.","A method that connects native code to Java code.","A method that is part of the JDBC bridge.","A design pattern for connecting two incompatible interfaces.","A"
"What does the `assert` keyword do?","It declares an assumption which, if false, will cause an `AssertionError`.","It ensures a variable is not null.","It is used to allocate memory.","It is a synonym for the `final` keyword.","A"
"What is the difference between a `HashSet` and a `TreeSet`?","`HashSet` is ordered, `TreeSet` is not.","`HashSet` allows null values, `TreeSet` does not (by default).","`TreeSet` is backed by a `TreeMap` and keeps elements sorted, while `HashSet` is backed by a `HashMap` and makes no ordering guarantees.","`TreeSet` is faster for insertions than `HashSet`.","C"
"In Java I/O, what is the 'decorator' pattern used for?","To add functionality to I/O streams by wrapping them, e.g., `new BufferedReader(new FileReader(file))`.","To create visually appealing user interfaces for I/O operations.","To secure I/O streams with encryption.","To compress data being written to a stream.","A"
"What is a 'memory leak' in Java?","A situation where objects are no longer being used by the application, but the garbage collector is unable to reclaim them.","When the JVM runs out of heap space.","When a program attempts to access memory it doesn't have permission to.","When data is written to the wrong memory address.","A"
"What does the `final` keyword mean when applied to a method parameter?","The parameter's value cannot be changed within the method.","The method can only accept final variables as arguments.","The parameter must be a primitive type.","The parameter is passed by reference instead of by value.","A"
"Which of the following is true about an `enum`'s constructor?","It must be public.","It is always private or package-private.","An enum cannot have a constructor.","It can be called directly to create new enum instances.","B"
"What is the primary purpose of a `Future` object in concurrent programming?","It represents the result of an asynchronous computation.","It schedules a task to run in the future.","It holds a reference to a thread that will be started in the future.","It is used to predict the future state of a thread.","A"
"Which stream operation is 'short-circuiting'?","`map()`","`forEach()`","`findFirst()`","`collect()`","C"
"What is the purpose of the `System.arraycopy()` method?","To create a deep copy of an array.","To efficiently copy data from one array to another.","To sort the elements of an array.","To compare two arrays for equality.","B"
"Which class can be used to format dates in a human-readable, locale-sensitive way?","`java.util.Date`","`java.time.LocalDate`","`java.time.format.DateTimeFormatter`","`java.sql.Timestamp`","C"
"What is a 'record' in Java (JEP 395, Java 16+)?","A transparent, immutable data carrier class that requires only the declaration of its state.","A database record retrieved via JDBC.","A file system record.","An audio recording API.","A"
"What is the 'head' of a `Queue`?","The last element added to the queue.","The element that would be removed by a call to `remove()` or `poll()`.","The middle element of the queue.","The maximum capacity of the queue.","B"
"Which of these is NOT a core concept of Object-Oriented Programming?","Encapsulation","Inheritance","Polymorphism","Procedural Execution","D"
"What is the `CompletableFuture` class used for?","A `Future` that may be explicitly completed (setting its value and status).","A future task that is guaranteed to complete.","A class for comparing two future objects.","A way to manage thread priorities.","A"
"When would you use a `WeakHashMap`?","When you want a map that allows its keys to be garbage collected if they are not strongly referenced elsewhere.","When you need the fastest possible map implementation.","When you need a map that is synchronized.","When you need a map that maintains insertion order.","A"
"What is the difference between `Class.forName()` and `ClassLoader.loadClass()`?","There is no difference.","`Class.forName()` initializes the loaded class, while `loadClass()` does not.","`loadClass()` is deprecated.","`Class.forName()` can only load system classes.","B"
"What is the output of `""1"".concat(""2"").repeat(3).length()`?","5","6","9","12","B"
"Which annotation is used to mark an interface as a functional interface?","`@Functional`","`@FunctionalInterface`","`@Lambda`","`@Abstract`","B"
"What is the 'module system' (Project Jigsaw) introduced in Java 9?","A system for managing external libraries like Maven.","A system for organizing code into self-describing modules to improve security, maintainability, and performance.","A new garbage collection algorithm.","A graphical user interface toolkit.","B"
"In a `switch` expression (Java 14+), what does the `yield` keyword do?","It yields control back to the calling thread.","It specifies the value that the `switch` expression will produce for a given case.","It is a synonym for the `return` keyword.","It pauses the execution of the switch.","B"
"What is a 'sealed' class (JEP 409, Java 17+)?","A class that cannot be modified.","A class that restricts which other classes may extend or implement it.","A class that is encrypted.","A class that has been finalized by the garbage collector.","B"
"Which of the following is true about `java.util.Stack`?","It is the recommended implementation for stack operations.","It is a thread-safe class that extends `Vector`.","It is an interface.","It cannot contain null elements.","B"
"What is 'JMX' (Java Management Extensions)?","A framework for building and managing Java applications and services.","A new XML parser for Java.","A Java-based messaging exchange protocol.","A tool for compiling Java code.","A"
"What is the purpose of the `Predicate` functional interface?","To represent a function that accepts one argument and produces a result.","To represent an operation that accepts a single input argument and returns no result.","To represent a predicate (boolean-valued function) of one argument.","To represent a supplier of results.","C"
"What does the term 'idempotent' mean in the context of an operation?","The operation produces the same result regardless of how many times it is applied.","The operation is guaranteed to be thread-safe.","The operation has no side effects.","The operation is irreversible.","A"
"Which of the following is true about `String.intern()`?","It creates a new `String` object on the heap.","It returns a canonical representation for the string object from the string pool.","It encrypts the string.","It returns the internal character array of the string.","B"
"What is the primary difference between `HashMap` and `Hashtable`?","`HashMap` is synchronized, while `Hashtable` is not.","`Hashtable` is synchronized, while `HashMap` is not. `HashMap` also allows one null key.","`Hashtable` is part of the `java.nio` package.","`HashMap` can only store String keys.","B"
"What is the 'Liskov Substitution Principle'?","A principle stating that objects of a superclass should be replaceable with objects of its subclasses without affecting the correctness of the program.","A principle for substituting algorithms at runtime.","A memory management principle.","A principle for designing user interfaces.","A"
"What is the role of the `java.lang.ref` package?","It provides classes that allow for interaction with the garbage collector.","It is used for reflection.","It contains reference implementations of core Java classes.","It handles remote method invocation references.","A"
"What is the time complexity for accessing an element in an `ArrayList` by its index?","O(1)","O(n)","O(log n)","O(n^2)","A"
"Which Java feature allows a method to accept a variable number of arguments of the same type?","Method Overloading","Generics","Varargs","Polymorphism","C"
